<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  FieldMini: the bed for the silent edge a failed NuGet restore used to open.

  ONE PROJECT, ONE PACKAGE REFERENCE, ONE USE SITE. JsonUser.cs calls a static method on a type from
  Contoso.Json, and the spec forbids types in this project from referencing Contoso.*. With the feed
  reachable, `check` reds at the use site; with the feed unreachable it used to exit 0 reporting the rule
  INERT — its target selection matched no types — because the package edge the violation rests on was
  never extracted. Not missed: never present. A restore that NEVER RAN leaves the identical hole with no
  assets file to read it off, and used to exit 0 the same way; it is caught now because this project is
  SDK-style and so would have written one. See RestoreFailureSilentEdgeE2ETests for all three arms — the
  third needs no feed and no restore at all, only this tree as it is committed.

  THE PACKAGE IS MANUFACTURED AT TEST TIME, not committed. The test writes a .nupkg into ./feed/ and
  points NUGET_PACKAGES at a private directory of its own, so the operator's warm global package folder
  cannot silently satisfy a restore this bed needs to fail. That confound is why "just break the feed" is
  not enough on its own.

  THREE SITING CONSTRAINTS, the same three BrokenApp.Web.csproj states, for the same reasons.

  1. It lives under Fixtures/RestoreFailureSolutions/, NOT Fixtures/TestSolutions/. FixtureRestorer
     restores every .sln under TestSolutions/** at pipeline startup, keyed on "any csproj missing
     obj/project.assets.json". A solution whose restore is the subject, sited there, would be restored
     out from under its own test — and, failing, would make every test process believe the whole tree is
     unrestored.

  2. It is NOT a member of SlideRule.slnx and is NOT ProjectReference'd from the tests csproj:
     either would try to build it, against a package that does not exist until a test manufactures one.

  3. Source only — no bin/, no obj/, no feed/, and above all no committed .nupkg. The feed is written
     into a private copy of this tree, never into the fixture.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
        <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
        <!-- No audit: an unreachable feed raises NU1900 warnings, and the detector's audit carve-out would
             then be deciding what the resolution failure is supposed to decide. -->
        <NuGetAudit>false</NuGetAudit>
        <RestoreLockedMode Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</RestoreLockedMode>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="Contoso.Json" Version="1.0.0" />
    </ItemGroup>
</Project>
